WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.275
Geographic range is one you can calculate, and the exam will ask you to.

2
00:00:05.525 --> 00:00:07.350
Look at the diagram.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.225
The light can see to its own horizon.

4
00:00:10.475 --> 00:00:12.575
Your eye can see to yours.

5
00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:17.975
Add the two distances, and that’s how far apart you can be and still see the light.

6
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:25.550
The distance to the horizon, in nautical miles, is one point one seven times the square root of the height in feet.

7
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.225
Try it.

8
00:00:27.475 --> 00:00:29.775
A light sixty-four feet high.

9
00:00:30.025 --> 00:00:32.525
The square root of sixty-four is eight.

10
00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:37.350
Eight times one point one seven is about nine point four miles.

11
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.675
Your eye is nine feet up.

12
00:00:39.925 --> 00:00:44.525
The square root is three, so your horizon is about three and a half miles.

13
00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:46.275
Add them.

14
00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:49.500
About twelve point nine miles.

15
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.050
And if the luminous range tonight is fifteen miles?

16
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.825
You still see it at twelve point nine.

17
00:00:56.075 --> 00:00:58.025
The earth is in the way.

18
00:00:58.275 --> 00:01:00.275
The lesser range wins.
